Solid principles are the 5 design principle which are helpful in the scalable, maintainable software devolopment.
S - Single responsibilities.
O - Open for extension and closed for modification.
L - Liskov substitution principle.
I - Interface Seggregation principle.
D - Dependancy Inversion Principle.

Single Responsibility Principle:
-----------------------------
1. SRP states that a class should only have one reason to change. It should be responsible for doing a single process but perfectly.
Bad Design:
class UseSrService{
void createUser(User u){}
void sendEmail(User u){}
void generateReport(){}
}
Good design:
class UserService{
void createUser(User u){}
}
class EmailService(User u){
void sendEmail(User u){}
}
class ReportService{
void generateReport(){}
}

1. Improved Maintainability & Reduced Risk: When a class has only one responsibility, it is less likely to break unrelated functionalities when changes are made.
2. Enhanced Readability & Understandability: Small, focused classes are easier to understand than large, "god" classes that do too many things.
3. Better Testability: A single-responsibility class requires fewer tests to cover all scenarios, as there are fewer dependencies.
4. Reduced Coupling & High Cohesion: It promotes loosely coupled code, which makes it easier to change or replace components.
5. Parallel Development: Smaller, separated classes allow different developers to work on different parts of the system simultaneously without interfering with each other.

