3. Liskov Substitution priniciple:
  The Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) states that child classes (subclasses) must be able to replace parent classes (base classes) without breaking the application. 
  Essentially, if a program works with a Bird class, it should work perfectly with a Duck or Penguin class, without needing to know which subclass it is using. 

  If B is a subclass of A, you should be able to replace A with B, and the code should still work correctly.
Uses:
---------
   Ensures correct inheritance
   Avoids broken behavior when using polymorphism
   Makes code predictable and safe
   Helps maintain design consistency

Bad Design:

class Bird {
    void fly() { System.out.println("Flying"); }
}

class Ostrich extends Bird {
    @Override
    void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Ostriches can't fly");
    }
}

Good Design:
-----------

interface Bird {}
interface FlyingBird {
    void fly();
}

class Sparrow implements FlyingBird {
    public void fly() {}
}

class Ostrich implements Bird {}
