Interface segregation principles:
---------------------------------
Interface segregation principle states that no client should be forced to depend on the methods that it does not use.

In practise, we break the fat interface into small and specific interfaces based on the client centric design

If you create one big fat interface, many implementing classes will:

Implement methods they don’t need
Leave methods empty
Throw UnsupportedOperationException()
Become confusing and tightly coupled

ISP fixes this by splitting large interfaces into smaller, purpose‑specific ones.
BAd Example:
------------
interface Worker {
    void work();
    void eat();
}

class Robot implements Worker {
    public void work() {}
    public void eat() {}  // ❌ Robots don’t eat
}
Good Example:
------------

interface Workable {
    void work();
}

interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}

class Human implements Workable, Eatable {
    public void work() {}
    public void eat() {}
}

class Robot implements Workable {
    public void work() {}  // Robot only works
}
 Benefits:
----------
1. Promotes loose coupling, 
2. Maintainable
3. Testable.
